  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 130 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nToday Through Saturday.\n\nSurface high pressure centered across the Upper Midwest will build \nsoutheastward into the Great Lakes region on Friday. Large-scale \nsubsidence within the building ridge axis will maintain mostly clear \nskies. A weak wave aloft will bring some saturation towards 15kft, \nespecially across the northern counties, so will bump up sky cover a \nbit, but otherwise expect only minimal cloud cover. The boundary \nlayer isn't expected to be quite as deep today, but very dry air \nwithin it will allow for another day of crashing dew points during \nthe afternoon hours with the potential for pockets of sub 20 percent \nRH. With the shallower boundary layer, wind gusts will be less \nfrequent compared to the last few days.\n\nOn Saturday, the surface high will shift toward the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast, returning southerly low-level flow to the Ohio Valley. Model \nsoundings from the GFS and ECMWF display a gradual increase in upper-\nlevel moisture, with cirrus filtering into the region ahead of an \nadvancing Central Plains trough and the eventual tropical remnants. \nWarm air advection in the 925mb to 850mb layer will boost afternoon \nhigh temperatures into the mid 70s to around 80. Deep-layer dry air \nwill remain intact through much of the daytime, keeping PoPs below \n10% through Saturday afternoon. Low-level moisture return will begin \nto ramp up Saturday night, with surface dewpoints climbing into the \nupper 50s as the pressure gradient tightens ahead of the approaching \ntrough and remnants.\n\nSunday Through Thursday.\n\nModel guidance over the past 24 hours has trended toward a slightly \nfaster and more southeasterly track regarding the tropical remnants \nof Hurricane Isaias as the system interacts with a mid-level \nshortwave crossing the Midwest. Recent runs of the GFS, ECMWF, and \ntheir respective ensemble means show the primary moisture plume and \nassociated moisture advection shifting east into the Upper Ohio \nValley and Appalachian region. Consequently, lower-level deformation \nand dynamic forcing over central Indiana appear weaker, resulting in \nlower total rainfall projections. Trough passage and a mid-level dry \nslot will clear precipitation out from west to east by Sunday \nafternoon. Highs on Sunday will range from the upper 60s to lower \n70s under lingering cloud cover.\n\nBrief mid-level ridging and surface high pressure will establish dry \nand warm conditions Monday and Tuesday. Return southwesterly flow \nwill push high temperatures into the upper 70s to lower 80s on \nMonday, falling back into the mid 70s on Tuesday ahead of an \napproaching northern stream trough.\n\nA more significant synoptic pattern transition is indicated in \nmedium-range ensemble guidance for Wednesday into Thursday. Guidance \ndepicts a deep longwave trough amplifying over the Central CONUS, \ndriving a strong surface cyclone through the Upper Midwest and \nsweeping a robust cold front across the Ohio Valley. Strong upper-\nlevel jet dynamics and deep thermal contrast will support robust \ndynamic forcing, introducing increasing rain chances late Wednesday \ninto Thursday. Ensemble ensemble cluster analysis indicates strong \ncold air advection behind the frontal boundary.",
